The '''French Belgian Sign Language''' ({{langx|fr|langue des signes belge francophone}}, '''''LSFB''''') is the [[deaf sign language]] of the [[French Community of Belgium|French language Community]] of [[Belgium]], a country in Western Europe.

It is closely related to the [[Flemish Sign Language]], with differences primarily in [[Mouthing|mouthings]],<ref>{{cite web |last1=ISO 639-3 |title=Change Request Documentation: 2006-001 |url=https://iso639-3.sil.org/request/2006-001 |website=ISO 639-3 |publisher=SIL International |access-date=2019-02-02}}</ref> but is generally regarded today as distinct from it.{{Better source needed|reason=Only one source is given, and it may be true, but the claim is that they are "generally" regarded as two distinct languages, and the single source doesn't clearly establish that. The Gallaudet Encyclopedia says that there is one Belgian Sign Language with regional differences, although it's from 1987, and maybe things changed since then.|date=March 2026}} It is distantly if at all related to [[French Sign Language]].{{Citation needed|date=March 2026}}

==Legal recognition==
By [[Decree (Belgium)|decree]] of 22 October 2003, the [[Parliament of the French Community]] [[Legal recognition of sign languages|recognised]] the Sign Language of French-speaking Belgium.<ref>[http://www.gallilex.cfwb.be/document/pdf/28210_000.pdf Décret relatif à la reconnaissance de la langue des signes]</ref>

